<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Etruria</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Etruria was the region of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> inhabited by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a>, bounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arno">Arno</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrrhenian-sea">Tyrrhenian Sea</a> and covering most of modern Tuscany with parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umbria">Umbria</a>. Its independent city-states, among them Veii, Tarquinii, Vulci, Caere and Clusium, flourished from the 8th to the 4th century BCE before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> absorbed them. Augustus later made Etruria the seventh region of Italy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/etruria.jpg" alt="Etruria" width="320" height="389" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NormanEinstein · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Etruscan_civilization_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
